{:edb/db-before-t 1203 :edb/db-after-t 1204 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534516 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:40:07.216Z" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161402 1078 #edb/ref 17592186161406 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161408 1078 #edb/ref 17592186161409 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161745 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(1\\right)$, calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right)$. Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161745 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(1\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161745 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/f3/f3440005b2b4e7ce1cc2eed5825d11cb309522a6807bcd18ffada189fadd0cda.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/1f/1f2f28b0f36aeb85a07076e1507c9561b707bf7d00de727998b02075b5458da4.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161745 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(1 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{e^{-x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(X < 2\\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 2) &= \\int_{0}^{2}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}e^{-x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-x}\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=-e^{-2} + e^{0} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.8647\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161746 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac 23\\right)$, calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right)$. Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161746 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac 23\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161746 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/49/49fa146efc8029727469bc5909c2a946c9cb96383976d4096d455b423a428479.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac 23 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/72/721ca1810b816431f99bb8294f4ad12ff125342640a925035487c8ecace4eff6.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x/3}|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161746 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac 23 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{3}e^{-2x/3},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(X \\geq 3\\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 3) &= \\int_{3}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{∞}\\frac{2}{3}e^{-2x/3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-2x/3}\\right|_{3}^{∞} \\\\\n&= e^{-2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1353\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161747 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(2\\right)$, calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right)$. Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161747 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(2\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161747 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e7/e77daa45c1026175eec99e71cb336ad5b8c2a46683170b0f9357a4ad7e9d1bf5.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 2 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e0/e0b6d1388350fabce97f26601d512e60cfe6ac3ab41230671718ca034c5c3860.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x}|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.3496\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161747 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(2 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{2e^{-2x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\frac{1}{2} < X < 2) &= \\int_{1/2}^{2}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1/2}^{2}2e^{-2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-2x}\\right|_{1/2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-e^{-4} + e^{-1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.3496\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161748 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(4\\right)$, calculate $P\\left(X \\leq 7 \\, \\bigg \\mid \\, X > 5\\right)$." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161748 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(4\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\leq 7 \\, \\bigg |\\, X > 5\\right).$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161748 1074 "Given a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t$, the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = 1-P(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 4\n$$\n\n$s=5$, and\n\n$$\ns+t=7\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s =7 - 5 = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 7 \\mid X > 5) &= 1 - e^{- λt} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{- (4) \\cdot (2)} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-8} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.999664\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186161748 1074 "Given a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = 1-P(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = 4,$ $s=5,$ and $s+t=7.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s =7 - 5 = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\,|\\,X>5) & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(4)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-8} \\\\  & ≈0.999\\,664.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424001 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424000 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424004 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424002 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424003 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186161933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424005 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162203 1050 840.0 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162685 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼Exp(2)$, calculate $P(X < \\frac{1}{6})$. Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186162685 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162685 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{6}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{0}^{1/6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2835\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162686 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼Exp(\\frac{1}{2})$, calculate $P(1 < X < 4)$. Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186162686 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ calculate $P(1<X<4).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186162686 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1<X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.4712\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186163182 1064 "A continuous random variable $X$ follows an **exponential distribution** if it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/2a/2ae9fdb32ab1b90d11729ead5966100d53b722ce182eacfb58ff9dc7d037993f.svg)\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and $e\\approx 2.71828$ is Euler's number. \n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows an exponential distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda).\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time between two events. In a future lesson, we will learn how to model situations using the exponential distribution. But for now, let's get some practice working with the PDF to compute probabilities." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186163182 1064 "A continuous random variable $X$ follows an **exponential distribution** if it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and\n\n$$\ne\\approx 2.71828\n$$\n\nis Euler's number.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows an exponential distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time between two events. In a future lesson, we will learn how to model situations using the exponential distribution. But for now, let's get some practice working with the PDF to compute probabilities." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186163183 1064 "Consider a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$, and two positive real numbers $s, t$.\n\nSuppose we're given that $X>s$, and we want to determine the probability that $X>s+t$. In other words, we wish to compute the conditional probability\n$P(X>s+t \\,\\big \\mid \\, X>s)$.\n\nBy the definition of conditional probability, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big)}{P(X>s)}\n$$\n\nNotice that $X>s+t$ implies that $X>s$, therefore\n\n$$\nP\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big) = P(X > s+t)\n$$\n\nSo, the above conditional probability simplifies to\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big \\mid \\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P(X> s+t)}{P(X>s)}\n$$\n\nNext, let's recall how to calculate $P(X > a)$ for some $a>0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > a) &= 1 - P(X < a) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\int_{0}^{a}λe^{- λx}dx \\\\\n&= 1 + \\left.e^{- λx}\\right|_{0}^{a} \\\\\n&= 1 + e^{- λa} - e^{0} \\\\\n&= e^{- λa}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can use this fact to calculate $P(X> s+t)$ and $P(X>s)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) &= (P(X > s + t))/(P(X > s)) \\\\\n&= (e^{- λ(s + t)})/(e^{- λs}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{- λs} \\cdot e^{- λt}}{e^{- λs}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{- λs} \\cdot e^{- λt}}{e^{- λs}} \\\\\n&= e^{- λt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that\n\n$$\ne^{-\\lambda t} = P(X > t)\n$$\n\nSo, we have proved the following property:\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\big \\mid X>s) = P(X > t)\n$$\n\nThis property is called **memoryless property.** It can be proved that the exponential distribution is the only continuous distribution with this property (note that the geometric distribution is the only *discrete* distribution with the memoryless property).\n\nIntuitively, the memoryless property states that the probability of an event occurring after a certain amount of time has already elapsed is independent of how much time has passed. This means that the distribution \"forgets\" what has happened before time $s$.\n\nWe'll discuss how the memoryless property is used for mathematical modeling in future lessons." 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186163183 1064 "Consider a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda),$ and two positive real numbers $s, t.$ \n\nSuppose we're given that  $X>s,$ and we want to determine the probability that $X>s+t.$ In other words, we wish to compute the conditional probability \n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big|\\, X>s).\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the definition of conditional probability, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big)}{P(X>s)}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $X>s+t$ implies that $X>s,$ therefore\n\n$$\nP\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big) = P(X > s+t).\n$$\n\nSo, the above conditional probability simplifies to\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big|\\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P(X> s+t )}{P(X>s)}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's recall how to calculate $P(X > a)$ for some $a>0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>a) & =1-P(X<a) \\\\  & =1-{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+{{e}^{-\\lambda x}|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{-\\lambda a}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can use this fact to calculate $P(X> s+t )$ and $P(X>s)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s) & =\\frac{P(X>s+t)}{P(X>s)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\lambda (s+t)}}{{e}^{-\\lambda s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\lambda s}\\cdot {e}^{-\\lambda t}}{{e}^{-\\lambda s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\lambda s}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\lambda t}}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\lambda s}}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that $e^{-\\lambda t} = P(X > t).$ So, we have proved the following property:\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\big| X>s) = P(X > t).\n$$\n\nThis property is called **memoryless property.** It can be proved that the exponential distribution is the only continuous distribution with this property (note that the geometric distribution is the only *discrete* distribution with the memoryless property).\n\nIntuitively, the memoryless property states that the probability of an event occurring after a certain amount of time has already elapsed is independent of how much time has passed. This means that the distribution \"forgets\" what has happened before time $s.$ \n\nWe'll discuss how the memoryless property is used for mathematical modeling in future lessons." 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186163185 1064 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(2)$, let's calculate the following probability:\n\n$$\nP\\left(X > \\dfrac32 \\,\\bigg \\mid \\, X > \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nGiven a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t$, the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2, \\qquad s=\\dfrac 12,\\qquad s+t=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s = \\dfrac32-\\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > \\frac{3}{2} \\mid X > \\frac{1}{2}) &= e^{- λt} \\\\\n&= e^{- (2) \\cdot (1)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{e^{2}} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 false] [17592186163185 1064 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(2),$ let's calculate the following probability:\n\n$$\nP\\left(X > \\dfrac32 \\,\\bigg|\\, X > \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nGiven a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2, \\qquad s=\\dfrac 12,\\qquad s+t=\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s = \\dfrac32-\\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{3}{2}\\,|\\,X>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(2)\\cdot (1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈0.135\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423964 1080 "1-3{e}^{-2}" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423964 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423964 1083 #uuid "00532a77-8369-5069-a645-da39692238cd" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423965 1080 "0.3654" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423965 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423965 1083 #uuid "09a50814-795d-5d28-8e92-209754c2af10" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423966 1080 "0.3611" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423966 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423966 1083 #uuid "20637901-6ccf-5b0b-8845-93cfcc926093" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423967 1080 "0.4150" 13194139534516 17592186045417 true] [17592186423967 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 